WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:04.940
What's the background to an artwork like this?

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.960
Where does this painting by Picasso come from?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.970
Could it have been expropriated by the Nazis?

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.590
Looted, stolen?

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.189
Every little detail on the back of the painting
is a clue.

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:23.119
To answer those questions, the Berggruen Museum
in Berlin has examined 135 artworks in its

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:24.119
possession.

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:29.499
The conclusion: none of the works were stolen.

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:33.860
We don't want any stolen or illegitimately
acquired goods in our collections.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.390
Even if it's a major work.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.640
If it happens, it must be returned.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.820
So far the Prussian Cultural Heritage Foundation
in Berlin has returned a total of 350 artworks

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.390
- and that might just be the tip of the iceberg.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.371
It's estimated the Nazis stole around 600,000
works of art, mostly from Jewish families.

00:00:53.371 --> 00:00:58.140
And many of them have still not been returned
to their rightful owners.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.780
... like some of the works found in 2012 among
a stash of 15 hundred pieces in the Munich

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:10.119
apartment of Cornelius Gurlitt, son of the
war-time art dealer Hildebrandt Gurlitt...

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:12.960
Pressure is growing on German lawmakers.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.049
Many think the process of determining and
returning stolen artworks takes too long.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:24.479
And opposition parties are demanding more
money be spent on restitution.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:29.770
I'm not satisfied with the situation because
in this case, action really speaks louder

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:31.119
than words.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:32.130
Action has to be taken.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.810
And if the museums don't have the funds to
deal with this, then we the government have

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.819
to see to it that we come up with the funds.

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:45.350
Visitors to the Berggruen Museum appear to
be on the same page when it comes to stolen

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:50.100
art: Germany has to work much harder to track
down looted works.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:56.689
The Gurlitt case really showed again how much
of a sorry state we're in where that is concerned.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:02:00.899
I think much more work has to be done.

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:04.689
We have to get our house in order on this.

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:10.010
If we don't, there's this nasty feeling, and
it's something future generations will always

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:15.510
end up being confronted with.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:22.260
For behind many an artwork, there still lies
a gruesome story of Nazi theft, expropriation

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.640
and injustice.

